The emissive failure is a real design bug. The default emissive texture is black, so the emissive factor multiplies zero and an unmapped material never glows. The glTF convention is white for a missing map, and that's what I intended, so I'll change the default. The normal-scale check is only too tight, since a flat map differs by a tiny tilt. The sphere normal failures are unexplained, so I'll replicate the generator in Python to find which vertices come out zero.
[94m[Bash][0m Replicate sphere generator to find zero normals $ if ri!=0: I+= [a,a+1,b+1]             if ri!=rings-1: I+= [a,b+1,b]     return P,I def sub(a,b): ret...
The zero normals come from the generator. Each pole has one vertex per segment, and the seam pole vertex at the end of each row is never part of a non-degenerate triangle. I'll rewrite the sphere with one vertex per pole and triangle fans, so every vertex is used. I'm making the emissive default white and dropping the now-unused black texture, and loosening the normal-scale check to a tolerance that fits a small tilt.
